  "notes": "The producer who built H.E.R.'s early sound. `confidence` is **low** — the producer relationship is sourced, the parametric detail is inference from the records. The drum instruction is a mix instruction and it is what the numbers hold: \"**soft programmed kit with a live-percussion overlay** — filtered kick on 1 and the 16th before 3, a low short clap or rim on 2&4, quiet 16th hats, fingersnaps, and long drop-outs under the bridge. **The drums are quieter than the guitar, which is the mix parameter.**\" So the velocity tiers are soft, the hat velocities top out at 0.44, the snare is a layered low clap, and the percussion is snaps and rims at −7 dB. He is one of the seven models in this section with an 808, and the entry places him at the boundary deliberately: \"sustained sub-808 with a long decay and few slides — **this is the point where this section touches `trap-soul` practice** — alternated with played electric bass on the band-tracked material\". Both halves are authored: `drums.bass808` with role **counter_riff** and a 0.04 slide probability keeps the sub while the bassline block keeps the played bass; role `bassline` would have deleted the band-tracked half. Harmony is the two families the entry names, \"played, not looped from a sample\", with real blues harmony where the guitar leads. The identifying instrument is \"clean, chorused, arpeggiated electric guitar as the harmonic bed\", which is the `arp` counter and the guitar-solo break in the arrangement. ⚠ Every drum number, the 808 values and every melody figure here are INFERRED. ⚠ The large half-time subset (snare/clap on beat 3) is marked [LOW-CONF] in the research and is not encoded.",
